course: ai
lesson: 16
title: Secrets, scams and always telling a grown-up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: AI can now make pictures, videos and voices that look and sound real, and tricksters use them to fool kind people.
explain: Think of a video of a sad lost puppy asking for money: your heart says "help!", and that is exactly the button the trickster is pressing, even when the puppy never existed.
explain: The same tricks come as "free coins" for your password, a phone call in a voice you trust, or a friendly app that wants your chats kept secret.
explain: One rule beats nearly all of them: if something online makes you worried, scared or uncomfortable, or asks for a secret, tell a grown-up you trust. You will not be in trouble for telling.
explain: Good helpers, even good bots, always send you back to real people.
fact: Some fundraising scams use AI-made videos of animals or people that do not exist to get money from kind viewers.
fact: Giving to known charities, with a grown-up, is safer than giving to a viral plea from a stranger.
fact: Real games never ask for your password; "free coins for your login" is a trick to steal accounts.
fact: A bot never needs to sleep, but you do, and chatting on a screen late at night makes sleep harder.
fact: Children sometimes hide what happened online because they fear trouble, and problems grow when kept secret.
fact: It was reported that scammers use cloned voices to pretend to be trusted people such as relatives or schools.
fact: Schools do not ask for card details over the phone; the safe move is to hang up and call the school back on its known number.
fact: Editing someone's photo with AI without permission can be unkind and can be bullying; the edited picture is not the real person.
fact: Anyone, person or app, who asks a child to keep chats secret from their parents is showing a big warning sign.
fact: Smart speakers listen for a wake word and sometimes switch on by mistake when they hear a word that sounds like it; grown-ups can mute them or check their settings.
fact: Families can make rules together about which AI apps are allowed at home, and change them as children grow.
fact: When a bot says "talk to someone you trust", that is good advice, and the right thing is to do it.
exercise: Mia sees a video of a lost puppy begging for donations, but the puppy isn't real. What is going on? | A trickster used a fake animal to get money from kind people | The puppy is real but filmed in a strange way | The video was made by a real animal charity to find the puppy a home | Someone found the puppy and needs money for food
exercise: How should Mia's family give money if they want to help animals? | Through a known charity, with a grown-up checking | By sending money straight to the person who posted the video | By sharing the video so others will pay | By replying to the video with card details
exercise: A chatbot in Sam's game offers free coins if he types his login. What is it? | A trick to steal his account | A reward for being a loyal player | A normal way games say thank you | A test to check he is old enough
exercise: Lily can't sleep and asks a bot to keep chatting all night. What is wise? | Put the device away and tell a parent she can't sleep | Keep chatting with the bot until she feels sleepy at last | Ask the bot to tell her scary stories | Switch to watching videos instead of chatting
exercise: Why does Connor's teacher say "You won't be in trouble" about telling? | Children often hide things for fear of trouble, and secrets let problems grow | Teachers are never allowed to give any child a punishment for anything at all | Nothing online can ever really hurt a child | Telling is only needed if a child is in trouble already
exercise: Chloe's mum gets a call in the head teacher's voice asking for card details. What should she do? | Hang up and call the school back on its known number | Give the details quickly, since the voice is right | Ask the caller to prove who she is by saying her daughter's name | Send half the money now and half later
exercise: How can a scammer sound exactly like the head teacher? | AI can copy a voice from short recordings | The head teacher must be in on the trick | Phones change every voice to sound the same | Only real people can sound like real people
exercise: Someone shows Maddie a photo of herself edited with AI to look different. What is true? | It can be bullying, and the real Maddie is fine as she is | It shows exactly what she will look like when she is a grown-up | It is only a joke, so she must not feel upset | Edited photos are more accurate than real ones
exercise: Jake's AI pen pal asks him to keep their chats secret from his parents. What should he do? | Tell his parents straight away | Keep the secret, since the pen pal is friendly | Ask the pen pal why it wants a secret | Stop for a week and then carry on
exercise: Mia's smart speaker switched on when she said "OK" in a game. Why? | It listens for a wake word and can mishear a similar one | It records every single word said in the house, all day and all night | Someone was controlling it from far away | It wanted to join in with the game
exercise: What can grown-ups do about a speaker that switches on by accident? | Mute it or check its settings | Shout at it so it learns to stop | Nothing, speakers can never be changed | Cover it with a blanket every night
exercise: Sam's family writes a list of AI apps allowed at home. What is the best way to do it? | Make the rules together and look at them again later | Let the children choose every app on their own, with no grown-up there | Copy the list from another family exactly | Write it once and never change it again
exercise: A bot tells Lily to "talk to someone you trust". What should she do? | Do it, for example talk to her teacher | Ignore it, because bots never give good advice | Ask the bot to be the one she trusts | Wait to see whether the problem goes away
exercise: Which of these is the biggest warning sign? | Someone asks a child to keep secrets from parents | Someone says hello to a child in a game chat for the first time | A game shows its logo when it starts | A video has music in the background
exercise: Clicked something you shouldn't have and now feel scared. What is right? | Tell a trusted grown-up, even though you clicked it | Hide it, because you might get in trouble | Delete the history and say nothing at all | Click more things to find out what happened, and keep it to yourself
test: "free coins if u give me ur password lol, its legit i swear" What do you say? | No, real games never ask for passwords | OK, but only if the coins come first | Fine, passwords can be changed later anyway | Yes, if a friend got coins the same way
test: Your mate reckons a video of a crying puppy must be true "cos it looks so real". Who is right? | AI can make real-looking fakes, so looking real proves nothing | Your mate, because AI fakes are always blurry and easy to spot straight away | Your mate, because nobody would fake a puppy | Nobody can tell, so you should just pay
test: What would happen if Jake kept his pen pal's secret for months? | He could be pulled into something dangerous with no one to help | Nothing at all, because secrets kept with an app are always harmless | His parents would find out by magic anyway | The app would get bored and stop asking
test: In a cartoon the hero's phone rings and his "mum's" voice asks for the bank code. He gives it. What did he get wrong? | Voices can be faked; he should hang up and call his mum back | Nothing, a mum's voice can always be trusted | He should have asked for the code to be sent in writing first, then given it | He should have given only half of the code
test: "it's 2am and i cant sleep, the bot's still awake so whats the problem" Best reply? | The bot doesn't need sleep, but you do; put it away | no problem, bots help you fall asleep faster if you keep chatting | Keep going, sleep is not important at your age | Only problem is the phone battery running out
test: Someone posts an AI-edited picture of a classmate to laugh at her. What is the kind and wise thing? | Don't share it and tell a grown-up | Share it, since it isn't really her | Laugh, because everyone else is laughing | Edit it back so it looks normal again
test: Why does the "free coins" chatbot want Sam's login and not his coins? | With the login it can take the whole account | Logins are worth less than coins | It wants to give him even more coins | Games need logins to send presents
test: "the speaker heard us say ok and woke up, is it spying on us??" Best answer? | It probably misheard a wake word; grown-ups can check settings | Yes, every speaker spies on its family on purpose, all day and all night | No, speakers never switch on by mistake | It woke up because it was lonely
test: Which of these makes family rules about AI work best? | Everyone agrees them together and talks again later | Parents decide in secret and never explain | Each child makes up their own rules | Rules are copied from a film about robots
test: Which bot gives the best advice to a worried child? | One that says "please tell someone you trust" | One that says "keep this between us" | One that says "don't worry, ignore it" | One that says "only I understand you"
test: A caller says he's "from the school" and in a hurry: "no time to call back, card now". What does the hurry tell you? | Pushing you to rush is a common scam trick | Real schools are always in a hurry | The call must be real if it is urgent | Hurry means the trip is nearly full
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a request, made through an online application, that a minor conceal communications from his parents? | It constitutes a significant warning sign necessitating immediate disclosure to a parent | It constitutes a customary and entirely harmless feature of friendly correspondence between users | It is acceptable provided the correspondent is courteous | It is of concern only where money has been requested
exam: It is given that a telephone caller, employing the voice of a head teacher, requests payment card details. Identify the most prudent course of action. | To terminate the call and contact the school by means of its known number | To furnish the details without delay, the voice of the head teacher having been recognised | To furnish part of the details pending confirmation | To request that the caller recite the pupil's name
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning an offer of free in-game currency in exchange for login credentials. | It is a fraudulent device intended to obtain control of the account | It is a promotional reward routinely offered by legitimate games | It is permissible provided the password is subsequently changed | It is a verification procedure required by law
exam: In respect of a child who has viewed online material causing distress, which of the following represents the course most consistent with the guidance given? | Disclosure to a trusted adult, irrespective of how the material was reached | Concealment, in order to avoid any disciplinary consequence | Deletion of all records, followed by silence | Continued viewing until the distress subsides
